  id: 'optimism', title: 'Learned Optimism', subtitle: 'Change the story you tell yourself after a setback.',
  description: 'Setbacks hurt, and the story you tell yourself about them decides how long they keep hurting. Learn to catch that story, test it, and replace it with one that is kinder and more accurate.',
  scope: 'For adults who tend to give up, blame themselves or expect the worst after setbacks. The course draws on Martin Seligman’s learned optimism, cognitive behavioral therapy (CBT) and Byron Katie’s The Work. It is self-help, not a treatment for depression, anxiety or any other condition, and it does not replace therapy or medication. If hopelessness lasts for weeks, if ordinary days become hard to get through, or if you have thoughts of harming yourself, please talk to a doctor or mental health professional. In an emergency, call your local emergency number.',
  outcome: 'A clear picture of your explanatory style, a pocket ABCDE routine, a thought record you know how to fill in, four questions for stressful thoughts, a worst–best–most-likely check and a nightly three good things habit, plus an honest sense of what each tool can and cannot do.',
